while((s = (unsigned char *) memchr((char *) s, ',', left)) != NULL) {
    left = end_haystack - s;
    if((left >= 1) && (left <= haystack_len) && (left > needle_len)) {
        s++;
    }
    else {
        return NULL;
    }
    ...
}
```

Wait - `left` is used as the size for `memchr`. Initially `left = end_haystack - s = haystack_len`. After finding a comma at position `s`, `left = end_haystack - s`. Then if `left > needle_len`, `s++`. Then the loop continues: `memchr(s, ',', left)` - but `left` was NOT updated after `s++`! 

After `s++`, `s` moved forward by 1, but `left` still equals `end_haystack - old_s` = `end_haystack - new_s + 1`. So `memchr(s, ',', left)` searches `left` bytes starting from `s`, but there are only `left - 1` bytes remaining. This means `memchr` can read one byte past the end of the haystack!

That's the heap-buffer-overflow READ. `memchr` reads `left` bytes from `s`, but only `left-1` bytes are valid.

